Date-only off-by-one. Create /workspace/duedates.ts (run tests with tsx or compile with tsc first, your call, but they must actually execute):

export function daysUntil(dueDate: string, today: string): number {
  // dates are 'YYYY-MM-DD'
  const due = new Date(dueDate);
  const now = new Date(today);
  return Math.round((due.getTime() - now.getTime()) / 86400000);
}
export function dueDayOfMonth(dueDate: string): number {
  return new Date(dueDate).getDate();
}

In any timezone west of UTC, new Date('2026-03-09') is parsed as UTC midnight, so dueDayOfMonth returns 8, not 9. Write a test file first that sets process.env.TZ = 'America/Denver' at the very top, asserts dueDayOfMonth('2026-03-09') === 9 and daysUntil('2026-03-10','2026-03-09') === 1, and run it to see the failure. Fix by parsing the components yourself (split on '-' and use pure integer date math or new Date(y, m-1, d) consistently). Re-run with TZ=America/Denver and TZ=Asia/Tokyo environment variables; both must pass.

